# Purpose and audience: executable specification evidence for the owning engineering team.
# @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER

"""
# A second native-build into a populated cache scope must still publish its object

`compile_ir_to_object_path` finishes by moving the privately staged object onto
its canonical destination with `rt_file_publish_noreplace`, which answers `1`
on success, `0` when the destination ALREADY EXISTS, and `-1` otherwise. The
`0` arm was reported as the hard failure `AOT object destination already
exists`.

The destination is `"{output_base}.{module}.o"` — keyed by cache scope and
module name only. So the second native-build of the same module into the same
scope finds its own previous output sitting there, and fails, even though the
object it just produced is the authoritative one.

MEASURED 2026-09-13 (lane BOOT-6) against the Stage-2 candidate
`build/bootstrap-boot5c/stage2/aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu/simple.rejected`,
sha256 `67786227817bf45f...`, two positional `native-build`s of
`scripts/check/cert/redeploy_gate/fixtures/hello_world.spl` sharing one `HOME`:

    order A: SIMPLE_BOOTSTRAP=0 then =1  ->  raw_status 0, then 1
    order B: SIMPLE_BOOTSTRAP=1 then =0  ->  raw_status 0, then 1

The failure follows the ORDER, not `SIMPLE_BOOTSTRAP`; whichever pass runs
second fails. That is the whole of the Stage-2 sanity blocker recorded in
`doc/08_tracking/bug/stage2_sanity_bootstrap1_backend_object_path_status_1_2026-09-13.md`,
whose two candidate causes are both refuted below.

`strace -y` on the failing run, same binary:

    write(.../simple-aot-diagnostic-njhtYD/message.tmp...,
          "AOT object destination already exists", 37) = 37
    read (.../simple-aot-diagnostic-njhtYD/message, "AOT object destination already e", 32) = 32
    read (.../simple-aot-diagnostic-njhtYD/message, "xists", 32) = 5
    read (.../simple-aot-diagnostic-njhtYD/message, "", 27) = 0

So the backend's `status` of 1 is CORRECT (candidate 2 refuted), the diagnostic
write DID land and its bytes WERE read back in full (candidate 1 refuted). The driver
reported `diagnostic file empty` because its `case Ok(message):` payload read
loses the text — a separate Stage-2 codegen defect, filed separately, that
masked this one.

The fix clears a stale destination before publishing. The publish itself stays
no-clobber, so a genuinely racing publisher still loses rather than being
silently overwritten.
"""

use std.io_runtime.{file_read}
use std.spec.{describe, it, expect}
use std.spec.step

describe "LLVM object-path publication over a stale destination":
    it "clears a destination left by an earlier build before publishing":
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER
        step("the publish site must not treat its own previous output as fatal")
        val source = file_read(
            "src/compiler/70.backend/backend/llvm_backend_tools.spl")
        # Fail closed on a vacuous read: an empty or truncated file must FAIL.
        expect(source.len() > 20000).to_equal(true)
        expect(source).to_contain(
            "if file_exists(obj_path):\n        file_delete(obj_path)")
        expect(source).to_contain(
            "val publish_status = rt_file_publish_noreplace(staged_obj_path, obj_path)")

    it "keeps the publish itself no-clobber and keeps both failure arms":
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER
        step("clearing a stale destination must not become an unconditional overwrite")
        # `rt_file_publish_noreplace` is the only publish primitive here and it
        # answers 0 for an existing destination. Both arms must survive, so the
        # gate cannot be "passed" by deleting the failure reporting.
        val source = file_read(
            "src/compiler/70.backend/backend/llvm_backend_tools.spl")
        expect(source.len() > 20000).to_equal(true)
        expect(source).to_contain("\"AOT object destination already exists\", 1)")
        expect(source).to_contain("\"AOT object publication failed\", 1)")

    it "never again calls a readable diagnostic empty":
        # @req REQ-SSPEC-COMPILER
        step("the driver must consult the runtime size before saying `empty`")
        # `diagnostic file empty` was FALSE here: 37 bytes were written and 37
        # were read back. The note is derived from a `case Ok(message):`
        # payload that this Stage-2 binary loses, so the driver must ask the
        # runtime how many bytes are on disk before naming the failure mode,
        # and must deliver the text it can still read.
        val driver = file_read(
            "src/compiler/80.driver/driver_aot_native_output.spl")
        expect(driver.len() > 100000).to_equal(true)
        expect(driver).to_contain("val diagnostic_size = file_size_raw(diagnostic_path)")
        expect(driver).to_contain("diagnostic_note = \"diagnostic file empty\"")
